WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.100
And today in this
presentation, I

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.590
would like to share
with you how to scale

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.620
Shiny to thousands
of users. My name

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.940
is Damian Rodziewicz.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.610
I am one of the founders
of Appsilon and I'm also

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.520
a technical person.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.990
So I work hands-on on a lot
of projects that we have.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.600
That's why I have a first hand
experience in scaling Shiny

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.500
applications.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.920
Be sure to leave your questions.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.270
I'd be happy to discuss them.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.620
And you can contact me later.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.490
If you have any further
questions to discuss.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.280
So let me start with a
very simple success story

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.210
that I keep seeing
over and over again.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.120
And I'm really
excited about this.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.120
Thanks to Shiny, you and your
team are able to build

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.870
a successful app very quickly.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.030
Sometimes it is just
a matter of days.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:51.150
And you start having your first
clients, customers, users.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.720
Very often these are internal
users in your own company.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.720
You are helping them understand
to better their process

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.730
and go through the
process much faster.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.920
And suddenly you
have more users that

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.930
are using your application.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.700
Very often we see
that people who

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.480
start using your application
love it from the first day,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.750
because the fact is that

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.800
thanks to Shiny, you are able
to implement new features very

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.410
fast, and Shiny is super flexible.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.390
You just make the
changes on the go.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:19.990
Everyone loves the application.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.570
And that's exactly what
the people want to do,

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.260
because this is a
dedicated software that you

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.033
built for them.

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:28.450
Suddenly you realize
that you have

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:31.220
a huge amount of users that
are using your application.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:36.070
And it starts
to become a problem because some

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.950
the reports show you that your
application is actually slow

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.280
or some will tell you that they
are blocked and they cannot

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.290
click around your application.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.510
And this is usually
when you decide

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:47.980
to seek for the solution.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.870
And these are
usually the questions

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.480
that we are asked: how
to make my app faster?

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.520
How to scale my
Shiny application?

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.630
How to build a scalable,
Shiny enterprise application?

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.650
And the one thing that I would
like you to first think about

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.200
is the fact that most
of those questions

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.500
usually start with
a question, "How?"

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.650
We are very used to trying
to find the solution as

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.460
fast as possible.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.530
We want to find good
tips and tricks.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.260
We want to understand
what's going on

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.900
and just apply new techniques.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.370
But there are two
other questions

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.680
that are even more important
that you should be asking.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:25.810
The first one is why?

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.890
And the second one is what?

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.310
Let me share with
you a quick story.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:33.370
There was a client that
had their car broken down

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.450
and they went to the mechanic. The
mechanic looked at the car.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.700
They realized that
the engine is broken.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.780
They took the hammer and
just smashed the engine.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.460
Then suddenly the engine
started working again.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.500
And the client asked,
OK, that's awesome.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.680
How much do I pay for
this simple service?

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.480
And the mechanic
says, you are going

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.190
to pay hundreds of dollars.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.270
And the client wasn't
happy about it.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:59.630
And they asked for the
invoice to see why exactly.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:04.030
Hitting an engine with a
hammer actually costs $100.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.340
And this is the contents.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.990
These are the contents
of the invoice.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:11.560
$1 for just hitting an
engine with the hammer.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.970
But the $99 is because
the mechanic understands

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:19.240
why the engine is broken,
understands what the root cause

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.770
is, and then knows what
exactly to do to get rid

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.350
of this root cause.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:29.800
And now before I jump
into why which I think

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.040
is the most fundamental question
that you should be asking,

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.220
let's talk about what.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.010
So that you have
the whole context

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.920
of what we usually
do when we want

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.870
to scale the Shiny application.

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.660
So when you think
about scaling Shiny,

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.340
there are two groups of
actions that you can make.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.220
The first one is called
vertical scaling,

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.750
and this is
increasing the amount

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.610
of users for one machine.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.120
And you can do it in two ways.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.040
The first one is just adding
more resources to your machine.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.370
So add more CPU,
add more memory.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.610
The second way
of vertical scaling

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.550
is making your Shiny
application leaner,

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.370
so allowing more
users to use it.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.710
And the second group is the
horizontal scaling, which

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.560
is just adding more machines.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.529
To put it in this
context, the first two

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:19.360
are actually fairly simple.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.060
I know I am a very
often asked about how

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.200
to scale Shiny horizontally, how
to have multiple servers with

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.550
Connect.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:28.660
Let me tell you this.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.748
This is actually
very, very simple.

00:04:30.748 --> 00:04:32.290
I'm going to share
with you resources

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:33.950
at the end of this presentation.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:36.940
So that you can take a look
yourself on the contents.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:41.350
But basically, the first simple
step that some people usually

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:44.860
do is just ask the DevOps/IT
if they can increase

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:45.950
the size of the server.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.890
They add memory, they add CPU.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:50.080
And this right away
gives you more users

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.550
that can use their application.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.140
Of course, you have
to pay for that.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.700
And very often it is difficult
to find additional budget

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.510
for increasing the machine,
especially if suddenly you

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:01.010
need.

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.468
So you have 10
times as much users.

00:05:03.468 --> 00:05:05.260
And you need to really,
really big machine.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:09.460
Then the second step that you
can do is talk to DevOps/IT

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.670
and ask for them
to add more servers.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:15.370
This is as simple
as just spinning up

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:16.970
additional virtual machines.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.420
If you are using Cloud or
a little bit more complex,

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.970
if you have like
physical machines

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.370
that you have to turn
on and configure.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:26.920
But the good news
is that RStudio Connect

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.090
is super easy to configure.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.560
There are just simple steps
that you need to go through

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.330
and suddenly all
of your machines

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.580
are going to run on Connect.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.550
All of them are going
to run your application.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.910
And the only thing it
costs you is actually

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.650
real money for the machines.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.600
Now, the third thing
that you can do

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.500
is the most difficult one.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:52.030
And the one that requires
you to understand why

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:53.530
the application may be slow.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:56.680
And this is for
you as an R engineer

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:57.980
to find the bottlenecks.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.880
And so to understand what
slows the application down

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.190
and to make the
application leaner.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.120
There are three main
things that you can do.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.860
Of course, there is much more
techniques that you can apply,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.830
but the key parts
are first to leverage

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.780
the front end, to use JavaScript.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.760
As you saw from the
previous presentations,

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.560
it's not that complex
and sometimes you

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.170
don't even need to
understand the JavaScript.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:24.250
You use the server
to generate HTML and send

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.050
it back to you when you make changes.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.530
It already saves you
very valuable time

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:33.430
of your processor. The second
one is to extract computations,

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.820
and this also decreases the
CPU usage of your application.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.980
If your application is
doing something heavily,

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.460
think about a way to extract
those computations somewhere

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:46.870
else, to leverage the ability
to use external services

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:50.200
and not put all of the pressure
on your Shiny application

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:51.220
server.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:55.300
And the third one, which is I
think the most commonly used,

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.280
is to just use a database.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.670
I have heard a huge
amount of success stories

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.770
when someone just
decided to move

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:06.760
all of his data or her
data into the database

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:10.270
and suddenly the application
was easily scalable, up

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.880
to hundreds or
thousands of users.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.100
This gives you two advantages,
which is less memory

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.440
usage and less CPU usage.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:20.740
So this is more or less
what you need to do

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.870
to scale your Shiny application.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:26.500
Now, I would like you to fully
understand why we actually

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.838
have to do this, because
when you think about it,

00:07:28.838 --> 00:07:30.880
RStudio Connect is a great
product that allows you

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.910
to scale Shiny applications up
to tens of thousands of users,

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:37.630
you might have
seen Sean Lopp's video where

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:40.040
he shows how this is possible.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.340
And also Shiny is very
fast on your local machine

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.160
when you just run it.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.860
Why should it be
slower for others

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:50.110
when there is just
more people using it?

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:54.520
So let me tell you a story based
on a very simple application.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:56.090
If you take a look at the code.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:59.360
And this is like a most
simple one that you can write,

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.262
there is a slider and there
is a text output. The server,

00:08:03.262 --> 00:08:04.720
the only thing that
the server does

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.690
here is the server
gets the input

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:10.330
slider value whenever
it changes and sends

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:13.850
the value back as output text.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.430
Now, what happens
behind the scenes

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:19.610
when you send such
application to the server?

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:22.640
The first thing when the user
connects to the server

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.450
is the browser is going to
download HTML that is generated

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:30.590
by your UI function,
CSS, Javascript, all

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.690
the static files like images.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.360
And at the same
time, server is going

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:41.360
to create a separate process
for you so that you can start

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:42.960
your Shiny session there.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.110
And you can execute the R code.

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:49.470
Now the thing about processes
is that R is single threaded.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:52.730
And you can specify
how many users you

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:54.890
want to have for one process.

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:58.940
But you need to realize that if
there are two users that have

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:01.950
the same process assigned
and since R is single threaded,

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:05.690
when one process does something,
then the other user cannot

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:07.040
actually do anything.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.020
I will show you
this in a moment.

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:11.090
But what you should
understand here

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:13.340
is that the server
creates a process

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:17.090
or adds the user to a
already existing process

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:20.840
to handle all of the operations
on your reactive graph.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.180
Now, the second
thing that happens

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:25.700
is the browser creates
a connection through web

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.530
websockets and through
websockets,

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:31.490
there are going to be
data and information being

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:33.350
sent back and forth.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:36.680
Websockets are slightly different
than the typical REST API

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.870
that you know, because
they allow you to have

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.217
a bidirectional communication.

00:09:41.217 --> 00:09:42.800
And that's why the
server can actually

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.200
push the messages to the
browser, which is not

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:50.930
that possible with a single
typical applications that

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:52.040
have a REST API.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:55.370
So this is the second step
is the browser actually binds

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.300
the Shiny inputs and outputs.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.500
And it starts the websocket
connection with the server.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:02.600
Now, let's say that you
are moving the slider

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:04.970
and you set the slider to five.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:08.180
The browser is going to send
this value to the server.

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:11.600
The server is going
to check this value,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:16.250
trigger the reactive computation
and return the resulting value.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.380
So in our example,
this is just going

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.890
to respond to the browser.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.140
The text is 5.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:24.660
Now, coming back to the
Pedros presentation,

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:27.920
if instead of just
returning a simple value

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.480
we return an updated
widget, then you

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.660
would actually have to
send the whole HTML.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.030
If you don't use
the update function

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:41.120
that Pedro was talking about,
if you used update function,

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.890
then the browser is smart.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:46.010
The browser receives only
the data that has changed.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:48.410
And in java, on
the JavaScript part

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.270
is going to update the HTML.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:54.570
So this is a huge value already
by using the update functions.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:58.110
Now, when it comes to the
browser and the server,

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:00.510
after all of the
computations are done,

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.520
the browser is just
waiting for another signal,

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.040
either from the user
or from the server.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.320
Now, this is a very
simple example.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.500
Now let's take a look what
happens when you actually

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:14.410
start doing some
difficult computations.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:16.770
So instead of just
returning the value

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:20.530
I'm going to perform a long,
complex CPU operation based

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:22.450
on the input slider value.

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:24.280
And now at the same
thing that happens,

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.670
you send the value through
the websocket to the server

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.450
and within that process
that is started for you,

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:34.750
the process is just calculating
the long running computation

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:37.240
that you're required it to do.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.340
And the problem
is that your users

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:42.700
are no longer is able
to make any actions

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:43.760
within the application.

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:44.860
Everything is gray.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.810
It is waiting for the output.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.290
And at the same time, new users
or old users that have

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:53.470
already been using this process,
also cannot interact with

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:57.040
the server and have to wait
for this computation to finish.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:59.170
And this is one
of the main issues

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:01.120
that we see in
applications when there

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:03.730
is plenty of complex
computations happening for one

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:06.400
users, the other
users are blocked

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.450
or the CPU usage is so
high that the other users

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:14.110
are seeing that things
are going slower for them.

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:16.780
So in order to get
rid of this problem,

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.770
you can use multiple solutions.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.890
First one, extract some
computations to the database.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:23.320
I will show you
this in a moment.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.630
Second one, you can use
shiny promises, which

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:29.950
is a great package by Joe Cheng
that allows you

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:33.340
to move those computations
to a completely new process

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:37.360
and to make the Shiny
free from any computation

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:38.860
you can use shiny
worker, which

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:42.670
is our package for
similar computations.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:46.090
Or you can simply move
some of the computations

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:48.490
to the Javascript,
to the front end.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:49.923
Now let's talk
about the database.

00:12:49.923 --> 00:12:51.340
This is the second
biggest problem

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.320
that I see in applications.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:56.380
You might create a successful
application that works locally.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:00.070
Fine, but in fact, the
application in order to run

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.440
loads a lot of data into memory.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:04.780
And we need to realize
that our computers

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:08.440
are similar to what
we have as servers,

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:11.500
they have their own ram,
they have their own CPU,

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:15.360
and RAM usually is around
sixteen, 32 gigabytes.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:18.020
It's not a lot, if
in your application,

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.160
you read one gigabyte
of data and then you

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.550
filter this data to do some
actions in the application.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:27.550
Let's see what happens
in the real life.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.230
You can see a machine here.

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:32.380
You can see five users that
are connected to RStudio Connect.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:36.040
And in this configuration,
we create one process

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.490
for every two users

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:39.170
that's accessed our application.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:40.340
This is configurable.

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:42.010
This is easy to configure.

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:44.680
But for this
purpose, let's assume

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:47.770
that we have to use
our spare one process.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:49.990
Right now, if you
have five users,

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:51.820
you already have three
gigabytes of data

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:53.680
being loaded because
every process is

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:58.060
like a separate box
that contains everything

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:01.120
that you need to
trigger all of the data

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:03.100
to compute everything.

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:06.670
And now when you have, for
example, 13 users, suddenly

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:10.150
you need to have seven
processes and seven gigabytes.

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:12.610
Let's see if you have 26 users.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:15.820
It already uses up to
14 gigabytes of data.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:19.180
And when you think about
it, 26 users is not a lot.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:21.400
So you should be very
aware of the fact

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:23.860
that the usage of
your memory is going

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:25.870
to multiply by the
amount of users

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:28.690
that you have in
your application

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.790
and you should try
to avoid loading

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:32.860
too much data into memory.

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:36.290
What you can do instead is to
set up a separate database.

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:39.440
And it doesn't have to
be a separate SQL server.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.480
It could be files on your
drive on the machines drive

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:45.460
that are accessed
in a different way

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:48.790
than just loading all
of that into memory.

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:52.210
You can check out to
search online for Christian

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:57.760
Egross' talk about different
possible ways of reading data.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:02.380
And even having a
separate package that basically

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.690
reads a lot of
files that terabytes

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:06.040
of data that you can search.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:07.480
This is just files.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.970
But when you have an
external database,

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:12.310
that database contains
this one gigabyte

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:16.330
and you just execute a filter
query and you get the result.

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:18.160
And here you have two gains.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:20.350
First one is, of
course, the fact

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:22.840
that you don't have to
load the data into memory.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:24.460
But the second one
also is the fact

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:26.950
that sometimes such
filtering is even faster

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:28.720
because the database
is especially

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:33.060
indexed to allow you to
make the queries very fast.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:38.010
So just to recap, when you have
a successful Shiny application, most

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.590
likely from the start,
because it is a prototype,

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:41.840
it is going to look like this.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:45.000
There is a server, there's UI,
a lot of communication

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:46.950
through the web
sockets and the server

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:50.430
is doing a lot of
computations by themselves.

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.780
The first thing to do, try
to leverage Javascript,

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.380
try to move some
computations to the browser.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:57.440
The second part, use
external server.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:00.930
You can use R API to create
a separate server that is going

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:03.310
to do the computations for you.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:05.460
And then just ping
the main server

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:08.220
when the computations are done.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:12.060
You can use the database, which
is the simplest way of already

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:13.980
giving you a lot of
edge, and then you

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:15.780
can just scale
horizontally by adding

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.840
the servers with your IT team.

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:21.300
Now, the other way to
think about it is you

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:24.780
don't want your application
to be a slow chess player.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:26.580
You want your application
to be the Forrest

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.620
Gump of table tennis.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.810
You want it to
just take the ball

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:32.170
and give it back, right away.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:34.680
When when the application,
when the front end

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:38.010
is asking for something, you
just respond very quickly.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:39.970
Hey, yeah, OK, I'm
going to do this.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:43.020
And the front end knows
that something is triggered.

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:48.520
Then you just delegate
your job somewhere else.

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:51.210
And once the job is
done at the front end,

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.670
know that something has changed.

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:57.220
This is similar to

00:16:57.220 --> 00:16:59.830
the comparison that I have in
my head, is that you don't want,

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:02.800
for example, your mother to come
into your room and tell you,

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:05.500
hey, now you have
to do your homework

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:08.290
and I'm going to stand here
and wait until you are finished.

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:11.150
You want just to say,
hey, do the homework,

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:14.290
and I will be back or
when you're finished.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:16.480
So this is the kind
of way you want

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:18.599
to structure your application.

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:21.490
Now, to sum it up, I would
like to tell you also

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:22.720
how to do this.

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:26.563
There is plenty of resources
that you can reach.

00:17:26.563 --> 00:17:27.980
We will share the
slides with you.

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:29.480
So we can click
through those things

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:32.980
and to see different articles
under a separate section

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:34.580
for leveraging front end.

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:37.180
There is a very nice book
about JavaScript for R

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:40.430
and there is a section for
extracting computations.

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:43.600
The shiny worker package,
shiny promises package, plumber

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:47.470
API, great, great
resources that you can just

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.810
jump into and start working
with. For using a database

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:54.160
I recommend using the reading
the main article from RStudio

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:56.260
that actually goes
through every step

00:17:56.260 --> 00:18:00.010
that you need to have the
database in your application

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:02.770
and to scale vertically
and horizontally.

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:05.830
There is a very nice
page about scaling

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:09.280
and performance tuning
for the dataset.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:11.860
That gives you an overview
of all of the configuration

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:16.090
options, especially how many
users you use for the process.

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:19.390
And there is a separate doc
about high availability,

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:22.060
how to scale your
application horizontally,

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:23.680
how to add additional servers.

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:25.960
As I said, this is
like really simple.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:27.880
Don't worry about
it, just try it.

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:30.760
You can even log into
AWS or Google cloud,

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:33.610
set up the virtual
machines, install RStudio

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:37.020
Connect and see how
easy it is to configure.

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:38.247
That is all from me.

00:18:38.247 --> 00:18:39.080
Thank you very much.

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:40.060
Hope this is useful.

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:44.330
And I'm looking forward to the
next talk by Marek and Fillip.

